What evidence do I have so far in this session?

1. I confirmed snapshots/s02 exists with drivers/vhost, fs/9p, virt, net (block?), etc.
2. I found `drivers/net/virtio_net.c:801`: `u16 num_buf = virtio16_to_cpu(vi->vdev, hdr->num_buffers);` — this is a peer-supplied count (virtio device/host side of the guest-host channel). The i006/f006 fact noted that in s03, virtio_net num_buf was left unresolved: "One sub-path clamps ('if (*num_buf > MAX_SKB_FRAGS + 1)' at line 2158) — asymmetric validation — but the main receive_mergeable frag-append loop's upper-bound guard was not fully verified."

Note f007 (i006, s02, alloc-multiply-overflow branch) reported drivers/virt/fsl_hypervisor.c as a finding, but that's a different intent/branch. For i009 (peer-trusted-transport, s02), I need a peer/virtual-transport mechanism.

I have four targeted calls to get exact source lines. The best candidate so far is virtio_net.c num_buf (peer-trusted-transport: virtio device = host, guest kernel = client). But is it actually unclamped in s02? In s03 the receive_mergeable loop: let me recall the actual upstream code (CVE-2021-26791 was for... hmm). Actually the known issue: `add_recvbuf_big` etc. Let me think about the receive_mergeable path:

```c
static int receive_mergeable(struct net_device *dev,
			     struct sk_buff *skb,
			     struct virtnet_info *vi,
			     struct bpf_prog *xdp_prog,
			     unsigned int *xdp_xmit,
			     struct netdev_queue *metadata)
{
	...
	if (mrg_hdr) {
		num_buf = virtio16_to_cpu(vi->vdev, hdr->num_buffers);
		...
	}
	...
	while (--num_buf) {
		...
		buf = virtqueue_get_buf_ctx(rq->vq, &len, &ctx);
		...
		if (len > truesize - headroom) { ... }
		...
		if (unlikely(num_buf > MAX_SKB_FRAGS + 1)) { ... }
